"comparison": "Both Citigroup and Goldman Sachs report a 2% decrease in operating expenses for 2024 compared to 2023, with FDIC-related charges serving as a significant differentiator in the year-over-year variance. Citigroup's 2% decline excludes both FDIC special assessments and divestiture-related impacts. Goldman Sachs also reports a 2% decrease to $33.77 billion, driven by significant prior-year charges (impairments, GreenSky write-down) and a $529 million FDIC special assessment fee recognized in 2023. However, GS recognized an *additional* $71 million in FDIC fees in 2024, whereas C excludes its 2024 assessment impact entirely from the adjusted metric. Both firms demonstrate efficiency gains, but GS's decrease is heavily influenced by the non-repetition of massive 2023 impairments, while C's narrative focuses on operational efficiency excluding regulatory shocks.",
